  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B046",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B051",
    "B012"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the strongest case because the magic-wavelength idea resolved a concrete quantum-control problem—holding many atoms without destroying the clock transition—and was followed by defining high-performance realization. The essential result is mature: exceptional precision has been reproduced independently, making this a robust physical method rather than a promising apparatus. It is also fundamental measurement physics, enabling stringent tests and new sensing without relying on a future standards decision. Katori and Ye remain a coherent, complementary pair for the conceptual invention and defining realization, provided the final historical review confirms that boundary.",
    "case_against": "The case is only as clean as its historical delimitation. The citation must not absorb the wider development of optical frequency clocks, subsequent implementations, or a possible future redefinition of the second. We must still establish whether the two proposed names alone adequately represent the invention and realization; apparent clarity is not a substitute for archival scrutiny.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that quantum error correction established the physical possibility of scalable quantum computation, despite the absence of a fully fault-tolerant scalable computer.",
        "response": "I accept the depth of B027: it changed our understanding of what may be possible in a decohering quantum system. But the missing sustained, scalable fault-tolerant realization remains material to timing, especially when its credit boundary also spans several foundational strands. B046 already joins a decisive physical principle to repeatedly verified performance in laboratories, so I retain it ahead without reducing B027's importance."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that lattice clocks combine Katori's magic-wavelength concept, Ye's defining experimental advances, and independent reproducibility, while warning against equating the achievement with the broader optical-clock field.",
        "response": "I agree, and the limitation is important. The prize should recognize the lattice-clock method itself, not claim all optical-frequency metrology or its institutional consequences. This narrow framing is precisely why the achievement remains compelling: it identifies a specific, experimentally tested way of controlling and interrogating many atoms."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson presents the quantum cascade laser as a mature and self-contained invention-to-device chain, conditional on clarification of the roles of epitaxy, concept, and first realization.",
        "response": "B053 has genuine scientific and practical weight, and its long experimental record deserves respect. I nevertheless find its proposed three-person chain less securely delimited on the supplied evidence: enabling epitaxy, the physical concept, and first realization are not automatically one prize-worthy unit. B046 has the more direct link between the defining concept, realization, and its proposed recipients, subject to the remaining archival check."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review confirms that Katori and Ye alone most appropriately represent the original lattice-clock invention and defining realization.",
      "How to draw a citation boundary that excludes broader optical-clock development while remaining faithful to the achievement's physical significance."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct connection to Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. My work and long-standing collaborations are in atomic and attosecond physics, including with Anne L'Huillier and related groups, but no relevant collaboration with the proposed laureates is identified."
  }
}
